definition:
    French physicist (1852–1908) who discovered radioactivity by accident in 1896: investigating whether uranium salts glow after sunlight exposure, he found they fogged a photographic plate even in a drawer on a cloudy week — the uranium emitted penetrating rays all by itself. Marie and Pierre Curie chased down the phenomenon and coined 'radioactivity'; all three shared the 1903 Nobel Prize in Physics, and the SI activity unit bears his name.
